D1: Do container metrics carry the node name?
timeseries cpu = avg(dt.kubernetes.container.cpu_usage), by:{k8s.namespace.name, k8s.node.name}, from:-30m
| filter startsWith(k8s.namespace.name, "app")
| fieldsAdd apmid = upper(substring(k8s.namespace.name, from:0, to:11))
| filter in(apmid, array($ClaimsApps))
| summarize nodes = collectDistinct(k8s.node.name), by:{apmid}
| fieldsAdd nodeCount = arraySize(nodes)


Check: each app has 1 or more node names. If k8s.node.name comes back null or errors, run this and send me the dimension names:

timeseries cpu = avg(dt.kubernetes.container.cpu_usage), from:-30m, by:{k8s.namespace.name}
| limit 1


I'll switch to another metric or the pod entity.

D2: Node Ready condition
timeseries c = avg(dt.kubernetes.node.conditions), by:{k8s.node.name, node_condition, condition_status}, from:-30m
| filter node_condition == "Ready"
| fieldsAdd last = arrayLast(c)
| fields k8s.node.name, node_condition, condition_status, last
| sort condition_status asc


Check: what values condition_status has (true / false / unknown). A node is healthy when Ready = true with a value > 0. If the dimension names differ, send me the error.

D3: How node problems appear in Davis
fetch dt.davis.problems, from:-30d
| sort timestamp desc
| dedup display_id
| filter event.status == "ACTIVE" and dt.davis.is_duplicate == false
| expand eid = affected_entity_ids
| filter startsWith(eid, "KUBERNETES_NODE-")
| fields display_id, event.name, eid, affected_entity_names
